You are correct.  Normally, there is a straight 1 in 10 chance the
hissing attack will start the stoning process.  During new moons, if
you do not have a lizard corpse, this is a 10 in 10 (read 100%)
chance.

What is confusing is that I haven't had any hissing attacks succeed in
quite some time.  I thought that was because I was carrying a lizard
corpse, but it appears that there is no sure-fire protection, even
during a new moon.

if(!rn2(10) ||
    (flags.moonphase == NEW_MOON && !have_lizard()))

The have_lizard check is paired with the moon flags check, so if
flags.moonphase is not NEW_MOON, the have_lizard check is not done and
the only conditional is !rn2(10).

That leads to another question regarding this:  suppose it is
NEW_MOON, but rn2(10) returns 0, causing the first part of the
condition to evaluate to true.  The second part would not be checked
(since the logical or need not evaluate anything else to determine
truth state of the overall condition).  Thus carrying a lizard corpse
during NEW_MOON isn't actually a foolproof way to avoid stoning. 
Wouldn't it make more sense to separate these into 2 separate
conditionals, the rn2(10) check being performed only after the
moonphase/have_lizard check performed? or is this the way TDT intended
it?

Or have I read something wrong here?  

